-- title:
--   Proposition 6 — if $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, the risk-neutral optimum dominates the newsvendor optimum and $\mathbb E[H_T]\le V_0$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds (the published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`): $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12); $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, and $D=\{(P,R):P\ge c,\ R-bP\ge0\}$.
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 1–5 hold (Assumption 1 for the laws of $A_T$ under $\mathbb P$ and under $\mathbb P^M$; Assumptions 2–3 at a maximizer $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ of $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)]$ over $D$) and the asset price trend negatively impacts demand, $C_T\preceq C^M_T$. Then
--   1. every maximizer $(P^{NV(M)},R^{NV(M)})$ of $V_0$ over $D$ satisfies
--   $$
--   P^{NV}\le P^{NV(M)},\qquad R^{NV}\le R^{NV(M)};
--   $$
--   2. $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)]\le V_0(P,R)$ for all $(P,R)$ with $P\ge s$, in particular on $D$.
--
--   It is the mirror image of the published Proposition 4 and is used in the proof of Theorem 3: the risk-neutral market is the larger one.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$ (published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`); reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$ (`IsMarkovWrt`); reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$; stochastic integrals with almost-sure integrability → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained for $t\le T$; the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` plus a pathwise drift integral; reason: values after $T$ never enter; under the augmented filtration the integrand classes agree up to indistinguishability (published base convention)
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the published parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - reading: $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ the solution of problem (2), named by Assumptions 2–3 → any maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold; reason: uniqueness is the content of Proposition 1 and is not built into a definition
--   - reading: Assumption 1 on the distribution of the market size $A_T$ → Assumption 1 for the law of $A_T$ under $\mathbb P$ and for its law under $\mathbb P^M$; reason: the paper applies the newsvendor analysis under both measures (Table EC.3; proofs on pp.ec49–ec57 use $F_M$ and $f_M$), as in the published base items
--   - encoding: $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, with $C^M_T$ the $\mathbb P$-version of the $\mathbb P^M$-law of $C_T$ and $X\preceq Y$ iff $\mathbb P(Y\le a)\le\mathbb P(X\le a)$ (p.12) → `UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T`: $\mathbb P(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)$ for all $x$; reason: the law of $C^M_T$ under $\mathbb P$ is the law of $C_T$ under $\mathbb P^M$; for probability measures the tail form is the distribution-function form
--   - reading: $(P^{NV(M)},R^{NV(M)}):=\arg\max_{P,R}V_0(P,R)$ (p.21) → every maximizer of $V_0$ over $D$; reason: the stronger reading, as in the published Propositions 4–5
--   - reading: the second clause, printed without the label “(ii)”: $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)]\le V_0(P,R)$ for all $(P,R)$ → stated as a clause of the proposition, with “all $(P,R)$” read over the decision domain $D$; reason: it is part of the proposition (it mirrors Proposition 4(ii)); every $(P,R)$ of the paper ranges over $D$ (p.9), and for $P<s$ the payoff decreases in the market size, so the literal inequality on all of $\mathbb R^2$ reverses
--   - generalization: $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)]\le V_0(P,R)$ for $(P,R)\in D$ → for all $(P,R)$ with $P\ge s$; reason: $D\subseteq\{P\ge s\}$ because $P\ge c>s$ on $D$, so the Lean clause specializes to the source's clause on $D$
--   - encoding: $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, presupposed finite → no extra hypothesis; reason: under $C_T\preceq C^M_T$ the negative part of $A_T$ is smaller under $\mathbb P^M$ than under $\mathbb P$, where it is integrable because $\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]>0$ (Assumption 2); so $V_0$ is the paper's value
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Proposition 6, p.23 (PDF 23)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Model
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Usual_UsualOrder

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV.Supplement

open HedgePSNV

/-- Proposition 6, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.23: suppose Assumptions 1–5 hold (Table EC.3, p.ec2)
and `C_T ⪯ C^M_T`. Let `(P^NV, R^NV)` be the solution of problem (2) (a maximizer of
`E[H_T(P,R)]` over the decision domain at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold). Then (i) every
maximizer `(P^NV(M), R^NV(M))` of `V_0(P,R) = E^M[H_T(P,R)]` over the decision domain satisfies
`P^NV ≤ P^NV(M)` and `R^NV ≤ R^NV(M)`; and `E[H_T(P,R)] ≤ V_0(P,R)` for all `(P,R)` with
`P ≥ s` (in particular on the decision domain, where `P ≥ c > s`).

**Formalization Note** `C_T ⪯ C^M_T` is `UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T` (the law of `C_T` under `P`
is below its law under `P^M`). Assumption 1 is imposed on the law of `A_T` under `P` and under
`P^M`. The second clause is printed without the label "(ii)"; it is stated. "For all (P,R)" is
read over the decision domain (p.9) and stated on the larger set `P ≥ s`, as in the published
Proposition 4; for `P < s` the payoff decreases in the market size and the inequality
reverses. No integrability under `P^M` is assumed: `E[H_T(P^NV,R^NV)] > 0` (Assumption 2) forces
`E[(C_T)^−] < ∞`, and under `C_T ⪯ C^M_T` the negative part is smaller under `P^M`. -/
theorem proposition_6 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (h1P : Assumption1 (M.P.map M.AT)) (h1M : Assumption1 (M.PM.map M.AT))
    (PNV RNV : ℝ) (hNV : IsNVMaximizer p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (h2 : Assumption2 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) (h3 : Assumption3 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (hneg : StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.P M.PM (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T)) :
    (∀ P' R' : ℝ, IsNVMaximizer p M.PM M.AT P' R' → PNV ≤ P' ∧ RNV ≤ R') ∧
    ∀ P R : ℝ, p.s ≤ P → nvValue p M.P M.AT P R ≤ M.V0 P R := by sorry

end HedgePSNV.Supplement
